<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>A fourth vertex of a parallelogram</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The points \(A({@A[1]@},\,{@A[2]@},\,{@A[3]@})\), \(B({@B[1]@},\,{@B[2]@},\,{@B[3]@})\) and \(C({@C[1]@},\,{@C[2]@},\,{@C[3]@})\) are three corners of a parallelogram.</p><p>Give the coordinates of a point D so that A, B, C and D, in some order, are the four corners of a parallelogram. (There is more than one correct answer.)</p><div>D = ( [[input:ans1]] , [[input:ans2]] , [[input:ans3]] ) [[validation:ans1]] [[validation:ans2]] [[validation:ans3]]</div><div>[[feedback:prt1]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Opposite sides of a parallelogram are equal vectors. For ABCD, \(\overrightarrow{AD} = \overrightarrow{BC}\), so \(\mathbf{d} = \mathbf{a} + (\mathbf{c} - \mathbf{b})\), which gives D\(({@g1[1]@},\,{@g1[2]@},\,{@g1[3]@})\).</p><p>The other two choices put D opposite C (parallelogram ACBD, \(\mathbf{d} = \mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b} - \mathbf{c}\)), giving \(({@g2[1]@},\,{@g2[2]@},\,{@g2[3]@})\), or opposite A (parallelogram ABDC, \(\mathbf{d} = \mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c} - \mathbf{a}\)), giving \(({@g3[1]@},\,{@g3[2]@},\,{@g3[3]@})\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
